DA-004 — Scene 08 — Martin Follows — Approved Draft v1.1

Martin waited until he could no longer hear Eli’s footsteps.

Without Eli in the room, Martin noticed the ordinary sounds more clearly: ventilation moving through the ceiling grille, rain brushing the window in uneven passes, water passing through the bathroom wall with a low intermittent rush. The charger remained unplugged on the dresser. The recorder lay dark beside it.

He looked at the door and checked the time on his phone.

Twenty-eight minutes.

Eli had said one walk.

Martin set the phone facedown on the bedside table. He had not meant to keep track at first. Eli was an adult walking an occupied guest floor with a camera and a flashlight. There was no practical reason to treat the separation like an emergency.

That had not stopped Martin from checking the time four times.

He sat in the chair he had moved earlier and listened deliberately. A door closed somewhere beyond the room. The impact reached him softly through the wall. An elevator motor started and stopped. Pipes clicked twice behind the bathroom.

None of it had the cadence.

Martin leaned forward with his forearms on his knees. He understood what Eli was trying to do, and understanding it did not make the experiment good. If something happened in the room, they still would not know where it came from. If Eli heard three impacts outside, he could not remove the other guests, the plumbing, the service spaces, or the ordinary sound paths they had already failed to map. If nothing happened, that might tell them nothing at all.

Twenty-nine minutes.

Martin opened the message thread on his phone and typed:

Come back.

He left the words unsent. Calling Eli back because he had grown tired of waiting would give him another reason to say they had ended the test before anything had a chance to happen.

Martin deleted the message.

Thirty minutes.

He stood. The carpet still held the old chair marks, one set no longer aligned with the furniture because he had moved it during the charging gap.

That mistake, at least, had a source.

He crossed to the dresser and looked at the recorder. He could turn it on, but Eli had stated the conditions before leaving: Martin stayed in the room, Eli walked the corridor, neither of them prompted for the cadence, and neither reproduced it. Martin intended to preserve those conditions because he wanted the exercise finished cleanly.

He went as far as the bathroom doorway. The light was off. The door stood partly open. He looked toward the wall where the earlier knocks had seemed closest, then stopped before touching it.

They had already tested that surface.

Martin returned to the bed and sat.

Thirty-two minutes.

A low thump sounded somewhere beyond the far wall. He looked up and waited. Nothing followed, and after several seconds he let the sound go.

After every isolated impact, Martin found himself counting the silence. He stopped only when enough time had passed that the next sound could no longer belong to it.

Martin had spent years telling Eli how easily people assembled sequences afterward.

Now he was doing it himself.

Thirty-three minutes.

One knock came from the hallway side of the room.

Martin turned toward the door.

The sound was not loud. It had the compact quality of something striking a hard surface beyond the wall or door.

He waited.

Ventilation continued overhead. A pipe gave a faint metallic tick behind him.

The pause lengthened.

Two knocks sounded.

Close together.

Martin stood so quickly that the chair dragged against the carpet.

He did not move toward the door at first. He listened for Eli’s voice, footsteps, the camera strap against a wall—anything human and immediate.

Nothing.

Thirty-four minutes.

Martin crossed the room and stopped with his hand near the latch.

“Eli?”

No answer.

He opened the door.

The corridor was empty.

Martin looked left, then right. No camera light. No movement. The nearest guest-room doors were shut, and the far bend blocked the section Eli had taken.

“Eli?”

Nothing came back.

His first thought was that Eli had returned, knocked the cadence, and moved away before Martin opened the door.

Irritation came first.

If Eli had done that, the test was over for a different reason.

Martin stepped into the doorway.

“Very funny.”

No answer.

A television murmured behind a door farther down. Air moved through the corridor vents. An elevator chimed somewhere around the bend.

He could walk twenty feet, look around the nearest corner, and settle it.

He stayed where he was.

The agreement had been that he remained in the room.

Martin went back inside and left the door open. If Eli returned, he would hear him.

A minute passed.

Then another.

No Eli.

Martin called him.

The phone rang once, twice, three times. No ringtone answered from the corridor near the room.

He ended the call before voicemail and checked for a message.

Nothing.

He typed:

Where are you?

This time he sent it.

Delivered.

Martin put the phone in his pocket and looked toward the open doorway. The knocks had seemed to come from the hall side of the room. That was as far as he could take it. He could not place them at the door. He could not prove Eli had made them. He could not tell whether the intervals had been exact or merely close enough for recognition.

One.

Then two.

His own stupid detail.

Martin stepped into the corridor again and pulled the room door nearly shut behind him without letting it latch.

He walked ten paces and stopped to listen.

The television farther down had gone quiet or dropped too low to hear. He reached the first bend.

Empty.

The corridor continued toward the service-side junction they had examined earlier.

Still no reply from Eli.

Martin called again. Four rings.

Nothing.

“Eli.”

His voice carried farther than he expected.

No response.

He almost started forward.

One knock sounded farther down the corridor.

Martin stopped.

His first impression put it somewhere ahead of him, but they had already learned how unreliable that impression could be. Door impacts had carried through walls. Elevator vibration had appeared in places that did not match its source. Sound moved through the floor in ways neither of them had mapped.

He waited.

The pause stretched long enough for him to feel his grip tightening around the phone.

Two knocks.

Farther away.

Martin stayed still.

Eli remained the easiest explanation. Eli knew the cadence now. He knew why it mattered. He could have decided that reproducing it outside the room would tell him something about Martin’s response.

Except Eli had explicitly said they would not reproduce it.

Martin looked at the blank phone screen.

No answer to either call.

“Eli?”

Nothing.

Martin walked.

Not fast, but he did not stop at the first guest-room door. At the second, a television produced a brief burst of laughter before returning to indistinct dialogue.

Human source. Ordinary enough.

He continued toward the service-side junction.

At the next bend he slowed. If Eli had made the knocks, following rewarded the trick. If Eli had not, Martin was still letting the sequence decide where he walked—the exact mistake he had warned Eli about all night.

He kept walking anyway.

The carpet changed near the bend. The corridor narrowed slightly where the wall jogged around a structural section. Beyond it lay the route toward the service passage they had entered with the guide and the staff boundary they had tested later.

Martin stopped before the turn.

A faint sound came from beyond it.

Human register.

For a second he thought Eli had spoken. The sound thinned before Martin could make out a word.

“Eli?”

No response.

He stepped around the bend.

The corridor ahead was empty.

Farther down, near the junction, a pale rectangle of camera light moved across the wall.

Martin exhaled.

“Eli.”

The light stopped.

A figure turned.

Eli stood near the service-side junction with the camera raised.

“What are you doing out here?”

Martin walked toward him.

Eli lowered the camera halfway.

“I heard something.”

“So did I.”

Eli’s expression changed.

Martin stopped several feet away.

“You heard the voices?” Eli asked.

“What voices?”

Eli stared at him.

“The knocks,” Martin said.

Eli lowered the camera another inch.

“Where?”

“Outside the room.”

“You were in the room?”

“Yes.”

“And you heard it outside?”

“I heard one. Then two. Hall side.”

Eli looked back in the direction Martin had come from.

Martin continued before Eli could attach anything else to it.

“I opened the door. Nobody there. I thought it was you.”

“It wasn’t.”

“I called you.”

Eli took his phone from his pocket. The screen lit his face.

“Two missed calls.”

“Yeah.”

“I had the camera running.”

“That doesn’t tell me where you were every second.”

Eli looked up.

Martin heard how the sentence sounded.

“I’m not saying you did it.”

“I didn’t.”

“I know.”

“No, you don’t.”

Martin stopped.

Eli held his eyes for a moment, then looked down the corridor.

“I heard something over here,” he said. “Like people talking. Maybe crying. I couldn’t get words.”

“Guests?”

“Could be.”

“Rooms?”

“I checked what I could hear from the hall.”

Martin waited for the rest.

Eli did not give him a child, a room name, or a ghost story.

“What did you call it?”

“Human-sounding.”

“That’s it?”

“That’s it.”

Neither man said children.

Martin took out his phone and showed Eli the elapsed time.

“You were gone almost forty minutes.”

“I know.”

“You said one walk.”

“I got turned around following the sound.”

“You didn’t get turned around.”

“No.”

Martin put the phone away.

Eli lifted the camera again.

“You heard the pattern twice?”

“Once outside the room. Then again farther down.”

“Same timing?”

Martin hesitated.

“Close enough that I recognized it.”

“Exactly?”

Martin looked at him.

Eli waited.

“No,” Martin said. “I didn’t time it.”

Eli nodded once.

Martin glanced at the camera.

“You get yours?”

“Not the knocks.”

“The voices.”

“Some of it, maybe. I don’t know what the mic picked up.”

Martin looked toward the service boundary. The corridor remained empty. Ventilation moved overhead, and a faint mechanical vibration passed through the wall near his shoulder.

He should have told Eli they were going back. The separation had produced two different reports under bad controls. Eli had heard distant human-sounding noise. Martin had heard the cadence. Neither event explained the other, and neither gave them a source.

That was enough.

Martin opened his mouth.

One knock sounded from beyond the closed staff door beside the junction.

Both men turned.

Martin stopped breathing.

Eli raised the camera.

Neither spoke.

The pause began.

